<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">FIFA World Cup</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1930">1930</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as World Cup</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The FIFA World Cup is the international association football championship for men's national teams, organised every four years by FIFA since the first tournament in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> in 1930, except in 1942 and 1946. It is the most widely watched sporting event in the world. Hosting it has become a source of national prestige, as for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in 2010 and for Qatar in 2022, the first host in the Arab world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fifa-world-cup.jpg" alt="FIFA World Cup" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kitzbuehel_slalom_ganslernhang_2010.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first international football match was a challenge match played in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> in 1872 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and the first tournament for nations, the British Home Championship, took place in 1884. Football was a demonstration sport at the Olympic Games of 1900 and 1904, and FIFA, founded in 1904, tried to arrange its own tournament in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> in 1906, which its official history describes as unsuccessful.</p><p>After the success of the Olympic football tournaments, FIFA, with its president Jules Rimet as the driving force, looked again at staging a competition outside the Olympics. On 28 May 1928 the FIFA Congress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> decided to stage a world championship, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>, celebrating the centenary of its independence in 1930, was named host of the first tournament.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Early hosts were chosen at meetings of FIFA's congress, and the choices were controversial because travel between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> took three weeks by boat. Holding the first tournament in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> reduced the European entries to four. The next two were both held in Europe, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and Uruguay boycotted the 1938 tournament in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, as the South American countries had assumed that the location would alternate between the continents.</p><p>The 1950 World Cup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> was the first to include the British football associations, which had withdrawn from FIFA in 1920 and rejoined in 1946. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>, who had boycotted the two previous tournaments, won it by defeating the host nation in the match called the Maracanazo. The tournament was expanded to 24 teams in 1982 and to 32 in 1998, which allowed more teams from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> to take part; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> finished fourth in 2002 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> fourth in 2022. European and South American teams still dominate, and the finalists of every tournament have come from those two continents.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Since the second World Cup in 1934 qualifying tournaments have been held within the continental zones under their confederations, and the finals are played within a host nation or nations over about a month, first in groups and then in knockout rounds. The trophy has been won by eight national teams, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> are the only team to have played in every tournament.</p><p>From 1930 to 1970 the winners received the Jules Rimet Trophy, named in 1946 after the FIFA president who set up the first tournament. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>'s third victory in 1970 entitled them to keep it, but it was stolen in 1983 and has never been recovered, apparently melted down. A new trophy of solid 18 carat gold, designed by the Italian Silvio Gazzaniga, has been awarded since then. The World Cup was first televised in 1954, and the 2018 tournament was watched by an estimated 3.5 billion people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “FIFA World Cup” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the most important in the world for international deals, German films won the foreign-language Oscar in 1979, 2002 and 2007, the Berlinale has run since 1951, Kraftwerk and Tangerine Dream pioneered electronic music, and the national football team won the <b>World Cup</b> in 1954, 1974, 1990 and 2014.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…859, is held by UNESCO to be the world's oldest university; Nawal El Moutawakel became in 1984 the first woman from an Arab or Islamic country to win Olympic gold, the national team reached the <b>World Cup</b> semi-finals in 2022, and Morocco will co-host the 2030 World Cup.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-morocco">Kingdom of Morocco · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its footballers won Olympic gold in 1924 and 1928 and the first <b>World Cup</b>, at home in 1930, and again in 1950 against Brazil, the smallest nation ever to win it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oriental-republic-of-uruguay">Oriental Republic of Uruguay · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Since 1994 South Africa has been an influential voice in African and global diplomacy, joining the BRICS group in 2011, hosting the 2010 <b>FIFA World Cup</b> and in 2023 bringing a case against Israel under the Genocide Convention at the International Court of Justice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Moroccan national football team qualified for the <b>FIFA World Cup</b> for the first time, for the 1970 tournament, the idea of writing lyrics for the anthem was proposed.</blockquote><a href="/songs/cherifian-anthem">Cherifian Anthem</a></li><li><blockquote>Today it is sung especially by Mexicans living abroad, and by Mexicans at international events such as the Olympic Games and the <b>FIFA World Cup</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/cielito-lindo">Cielito Lindo</a></li></ul></section>
